Python's standard library in unityrs, and the changes made to it
----------------------------------------------------------------

These files of the unityrs repository translate code of Python 3.11's
standard library (CPython 3.11) to Rust:
- crates/unityrs-core/src/detect.rs: Lib/gzip.py's _read_exact,
  _read_gzip_header, _GzipReader.read and _GzipReader._read_eof, how gzip
  detection reads each member's header, inflates it and checks its trailer;
- crates/unityrs-core/src/pystr.rs: Lib/ntpath.py's splitdrive and split
  (for basename), and Lib/genericpath.py's _splitext (for splitext);
- crates/unityrs-dump/src/select/glob.rs: Lib/fnmatch.py's translate, and the
  character set of Lib/re/_parser.py's _parse;
- crates/unityrs-dump/src/select/index.rs: Modules/_json.c's
  scan_once_unicode, _match_number_unicode, scanstring_unicode,
  _parse_array_unicode and _parse_object_unicode;
- crates/unityrs-cli/src/args/mod.rs: Lib/argparse.py's
  ArgumentParser._parse_known_args (with its consume_optional),
  _parse_optional, _get_option_tuples and _negative_number_matcher.

The changes made to them: each is rewritten in Rust and keeps only what
unityrs uses of it. The path rules work on bytes, the glob on code points
and the JSON reader on the index's UTF-8 text, where Python works on str
objects. A failure is an error value where Python raises. The gzip reader
keeps only the first 20 bytes it inflates and the length of the whole, and
stops at unityrs's inflate bound and the open's work. The glob is matched by
a wildcard matcher where Python compiles a regular expression. The JSON
reader builds the values an index holds, as Python's json.loads does. The
argument parser parses the dump's own options, with unityrs's extensions.
